rt2 = llvmorg-23.1.1 + the six patches ARM32 Windows still needs

Generated from the two source trees, not written by hand, and verified by applying it to a
fresh extraction of llvmorg-23.1.1 and comparing the result to rt2's source file for file.

  0002  lld/COFF: a locally defined dllimport function got its __imp_ pointer written with
        bit 0 clear, so an indirect call through the slot requested ARM state on a
        Thumb-only device.
  0003  ARM: registers forwarded through a musttail call were not modelled as live, so a
        vcall thunk could use an argument register as scratch.
  0004  MC/CodeView: a tuple-register DBG_VALUE aborted the backend with "unknown codeview
        register".  Re-anchored for 23.x, which spells the parameter Reg and uses Reg.id().
  0005  Tests for the this-adjusting thunk cases in 0003.
  0007  ARM: a musttail vararg forwarder that also calls va_start aborted the backend.
        THREE of rt1's four hunks are deliberately absent: they thread an isMustTail
        parameter through IsEligibleForTailCallOptimization, and 23.1.1 passes
        CallLoweringInfo into that function instead, so CLI.CB is already in scope. The
        surviving hunk is also tightened -- the exemption fires only when the argument-register
        save area has no byval contribution, because 23 grew a separate byval tail-call path
        (ByValNeedsCopyForTailCall) that 18.x did not have.
  0010  clang: _setjmp on Windows ARM32 was handed the frame pointer instead of the entry
        stack pointer, so longjmp died STATUS_BAD_STACK.

The original 18.x-targeted patches, which carry the full defect analysis, are in
patches/origin/. They are NOT what rt2 was built from; this file is.

